(4768905, 594033, 50034, '<td valign="top">For one I would welcome a lazy cat...that is not bulimic.  Our oldest cat, Monaco, walked into our lives back in January of 1995.  So, that would make her about 13 1/2 or 14 years old by now.  (We guessed that she was about six to nine months...or maybe a year old when she was "dropped off" on our dirt road.)<br/> <br/>Anyhow, "Coco" as well like to call her now eats uncontrollably...snarfing down everything available, then spends the next half hour purging.  (It has really gotten old...)  We have tried feeding her separately from the other two cats knowing that if we feed them normally and feed her as though she was on a subsistence diet, then she will have a "progressive supper" every time she eats.<br/> <br/>Yet, this still causes me to have to monitor the amount of water she gets right after eating or she still purges.  Suggestions anyone?<br/> <br/>Frankly, I never imagined that I would end up being a feline hospice nurse...  :}</td>', 'For one I would welcome a lazy cat...that is not bulimic.  Our oldest cat, Monaco, walked into our lives back in January of 1995.  So, that would make her about 13 1/2 or 14 years old by now.  (We guessed that she was about six to nine months...or maybe a year old when she was "dropped off" on our dirt road.) Anyhow, "Coco" as well like to call her now eats uncontrollably...snarfing down everything available, then spends the next half hour purging.  (It has really gotten old...)  We have tried feeding her separately from the other two cats knowing that if we feed them normally and feed her as though she was on a subsistence diet, then she will have a "progressive supper" every time she eats. Yet, this still causes me to have to monitor the amount of water she gets right after eating or she still purges.  Suggestions anyone? Frankly, I never imagined that I would end up being a feline hospice nurse...  :}', '2008-06-14 21:07:00.000000', NULL, 0),
